A motion generator may be written by inheriting from dls::MotionGenerator. It
communicates with the rest of the framework by reading a `dls::BlindState` using
`dls::MotionGenerator::readBlindStateSignal` and publishes a `dls::GaitSignal`
using the function `dls::MotionGenerator::publishData`.

The motion generator is made available to the rest of the framework by exporting
it using the macro `DLS_EXPORT_MOTION_GENERATOR`.

As a simple example, consider the following class:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_MOTION_GENERATOR_HPP@
```

Its implementation is shown here:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_MOTION_GENERATOR_CPP@
```

The framework will first look for the motion generator in the directory where the
framework was launched. This means that, during development and debugging, a
generator can be recompiled _while the framework is running_ and reloaded.

The framework will also look in predetermined installation directories for the
motion generator. To install it to the correct location automatically, even if
these directories were to change in future, use the following CMake code:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_MOTION_GENERATOR_CMAKE@
```
